[Black "King's Gambit 3.Bc4 Nc6 4.Nf3 #01"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient and faced with precise Black's play, White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. } exf4 3. Bc4 Nc6 $1 { This move, according to John Shaw, an author of a massive book on the King's Gambit, is the refutation of the Bishop's Gambit. By 'refutation' he doesn't mean that Black wins, but that he achieves a better position in all variations. I think he is right in principle, but many of the specific lines he included in his book can be improved upon. } 4. Nf3 $6 { It's difficult to say that already this natural move is dubious but in fact this is the case. Black has a relatively simple way to obtain a really big advantage. -KEY- } g5 5. Nc3 $2 { Shaw implies that Black should steer clear of accepting the sacrifice, but I see no reason for such caution. } g4 $1 6. O-O gxf3 7. Qxf3 Qh4 $19 { Threatening ...Bc5+ and ...Ne5. Black is simply winning. } 
