1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient and faced with precise Black's play, White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. } exf4 3. Bc4 Nc6 $1 { This move, according to John Shaw, an author of a massive book on the King's Gambit, is the refutation of the Bishop's Gambit. By 'refutation' he doesn't mean that Black wins, but that he achieves a better position in all variations. I think he is right in principle, but many of the specific lines he included in his book can be improved upon. } 4. Nf3 $6 { It's difficult to say that already this natural move is dubious but in fact this is the case. Black has a relatively simple way to obtain a really big advantage. } g5 5. O-O { -KEY- } Bg7 6. d4 d6 7. c3 h6 8. g3 $2 { This is based on one trick which just doesn't work. } fxg3 ( 8... Bh3 { is also good, but unnecessary. } ) 
9. Bxf7+ { This sacrifice may seem tempting, but it simply loses by force. } Kxf7 10. Ne5+ Ke7 ( { Of course not } 10... Ke8 $4 11. Qh5 { and mate. } ) 
11. Rf7+ Ke8 12. Qh5 { It all looks dangerous but you shouldn't be afraid if it's just winning for Black. } Nxe5 13. dxe5 Bg4 $1 { the key trick which allows the king to escape. } 14. Qg6 Ne7 15. Qxg7 gxh2+ { Don't forget to take the pawn with a check! This move could have been inserted earlier too. } 16. Kxh2 Kd7 $19 { White is busted. } 
 *
